# Universal TypeScript Rules — GoodEpoch Cursor Rules Pack v1
# Applies to: Any TypeScript project
# Compatible with: React 19, Next.js 15, Node.js 22, TypeScript 5.5+

## TypeScript Fundamentals

- Use TypeScript 5.5+ features: strict mode, `noUncheckedIndexedAccess`, `exactOptionalPropertyTypes` enabled in tsconfig
- Never use `any`. Use `unknown` for truly unknown types, then narrow with type guards
- Prefer `type` over `interface` for object shapes unless declaration merging is needed
- Use `satisfies` operator to validate object literals against a type without widening
- Always define return types on exported functions
- Use `const` assertions (`as const`) for literal types and readonly arrays
- Discriminated unions over optional properties: `{ kind: 'loading' } | { kind: 'error', error: Error } | { kind: 'data', data: T }`

## File and Module Conventions

- One primary export per file. Named exports preferred over default exports (except pages/layouts)
- Co-locate related files: `feature/Component.tsx`, `feature/Component.test.tsx`, `feature/Component.types.ts`
- Index barrel files only at package/feature boundaries, not deeply nested
- Import order: external packages → internal aliases → relative imports → types
- Use path aliases (`@/`) for internal imports, never `../../../`

## Naming Conventions

- Components: PascalCase (`UserProfile`, `AuthButton`)
- Hooks: `use` prefix, camelCase (`useUser`, `useFormState`)
- Utilities/helpers: camelCase (`formatDate`, `parseConfig`)
- Constants: SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE for module-level, camelCase for local
- Types/interfaces: PascalCase, no `I` prefix, no `Type` suffix (`User`, `Config`, `ApiResponse`)
- Event handlers: `handle` prefix (`handleSubmit`, `handleClick`, `handleChange`)
- Boolean variables: `is`, `has`, `can`, `should` prefix (`isLoading`, `hasError`, `canSubmit`)

## Error Handling

- Use `Result<T, E>` pattern for operations that can fail predictably:
  ```typescript
  type Result<T, E = Error> = { ok: true; value: T } | { ok: false; error: E }
  ```
- Never swallow errors with empty catch blocks
- Always log errors with context before rethrowing
- Use custom error classes that extend `Error` with structured metadata
- For async operations, always handle both the happy path and error path explicitly

## Async and Promises

- Use `async/await` over `.then()/.catch()` chains
- Never use `Promise.all` with side effects that can fail partially without rollback
- Use `Promise.allSettled` when you need all results regardless of failure
- For parallel independent fetches: `const [a, b] = await Promise.all([fetchA(), fetchB()])`
- Avoid async in useEffect — create an inner async function and call it

## Functions and Logic

- Functions should do one thing. If you can't describe it in one sentence, split it
- Max 3 levels of nesting. Extract early returns or helper functions beyond that
- Prefer pure functions — same input, same output, no side effects
- Default parameters over `|| fallback` patterns: `function foo(x = 0)` not `function foo(x) { x = x || 0 }`
- Destructure objects at parameter level: `function user({ id, name }: User)` not `function user(user: User)`

## React Fundamentals (all frameworks)

- Function components only. Zero class components
- Hooks at the top of the component, never conditional
- Extract custom hooks for any logic that involves state + effects
- Memoize sparingly: only when profiling shows a problem. `useMemo`/`useCallback` are not free
- Key prop must be stable and unique — never array index for dynamic lists
- Avoid prop drilling beyond 2 levels — use composition, context, or state library

## State Management

- Prefer URL state (search params) for filterable/shareable UI state
- Prefer server state (React Query / SWR / server components) over client state for async data
- `useState` for truly local, ephemeral UI state only
- `useReducer` when state transitions have business logic
- Zustand for global client state if needed — never Redux in 2026

## Security

- Never interpolate user input into SQL — always parameterized queries
- Sanitize and validate ALL inputs at the server boundary (zod preferred)
- Never log sensitive data (passwords, tokens, PII)
- Store secrets in environment variables, never in code or version control
- Use `crypto.randomUUID()` for IDs, not `Math.random()`

## Comments and Documentation

- Comments explain WHY, not WHAT. The code shows what; comments explain intent and decisions
- Add JSDoc only on public APIs and utility functions
- Mark workarounds with `// TODO(#issue): reason` — include issue number
- Remove console.log before committing. Use structured logging in production

## Performance

- Measure before optimizing. Profile first
- Lazy-load heavy dependencies: `const { heavyFn } = await import('./heavy')`
- Avoid creating objects/arrays in render hot paths
- Prefer CSS over JS for animations

## Testing Mindset

- Test behavior, not implementation
- Prefer integration tests over unit tests for UI
- Mock at the network layer (MSW), not the module layer
- Every bug gets a regression test before the fix
